WEBVTT

NOTE This transcript is the episode script as written and fact-checked, timed to the recording. Timestamps are approximate within a sentence.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.300
What's up guys, welcome back to CrimeTimeSnacks.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:13.200
A missing twelve-year-old girl's mummified remains were discovered nearly two months later in an abandoned Florida hog shed.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.600
That's where this story ends.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.900
Today we're going back to the beginning — to understand how it got there.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.400
Ted Bundy is believed to have murdered at least 30 young women between 1974 and 1978.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.100
His crimes spanned Washington, Oregon, Utah, Colorado, Idaho, and Florida.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.200
That's not a spree.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.700
That's a campaign, carried out over four years, across half a dozen states.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.100
The modus operandi was methodical.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:43.400
Bundy would approach his victims while wearing an arm sling or cast, using a false pretext — asking someone to help him carry books, for example.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:51.600
Once he had them close to his Volkswagen Beetle, he'd bludgeon them unconscious, typically with a crowbar, then drive them to a pre-selected secluded location.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.600
What the evidence actually shows is a man who understood how to weaponize trust.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.000
He looked approachable.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.000
He asked for help.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.900
And by the time a victim understood what was happening, it was too late.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:09.500
That pattern held across state lines, across years, and ultimately, it held in Lake City, Florida, in February 1978.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.100
Let's unpack it.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.400
The timeline leading up to Lake City is crucial.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.400
In early 1977, Bundy was in custody in Colorado, facing a murder charge.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.300
He was acting as his own attorney.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.300
That gave him access to the law library at the Pitkin County Courthouse.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.300
On June 7, 1977, he jumped from a second-story window while the guard was on a smoke break.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.200
He vanished into the mountains for six days before being recaptured.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.500
Six months later, he did it again.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:41.800
On December 30, 1977, he slipped through a gap in the ceiling of his cell at the Garfield County Jail in Glenwood Springs.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.400
He boarded a flight to Chicago, took a train to Michigan, drove to Atlanta, and caught a bus to Tallahassee.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.400
He made it to Tallahassee before anyone knew he was gone.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.000
He was added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list on February 10, 1978, over a month after his escape.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.400
That brings us to February 9, 1978.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.700
Kimberly Diane Leach was twelve years old.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.200
She was in the seventh grade.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.800
That morning, she vanished from her junior high school in Lake City.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.100
The case file shows that multiple eyewitnesses came forward.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.900
They reported seeing a white van circling the school.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.500
More importantly, they saw an adult man leading a young girl to that van and driving off.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Six days after Kimberly disappeared, a police officer in Pensacola spotted a suspicious car at 1:30 in the morning.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:23.900
He ran the plates.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.700
The orange Volkswagen was stolen.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.200
After a brief scuffle, the driver was arrested.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.400
He refused to give his real name, handing over a stolen driver's license and claiming he was an FSU student named Kenneth.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.000
It took two days in custody before he finally revealed his true identity to authorities.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.400
Investigators quickly connected him to the disappearance in Lake City.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.500
They found a stolen white van tied to him.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.700
Fiber analysis showed a two-way transfer.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.300
Analysts found fibers matching Kimberly's clothing inside the van.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.000
Fibers from his clothing were later found on her body.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.400
The physical evidence was locking into place.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:05.200
To understand how a man ends up driving a stolen car through Pensacola with a fake ID, we have to look at where the timeline actually starts.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.100
The record points to early 1974.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:12.900
That winter, young women began disappearing at an alarming rate across the state of Washington.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.400
The first was a twenty-one-year-old undergraduate at the University of Washington named Lynda Ann Healy.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.800
On February 1, she vanished from her basement room in Seattle.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.700
The case file shows this was the only disappearance in the early cluster that left behind physical evidence.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:33.500
Investigators found a blood-stained mattress and a nightgown left behind in her room.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Just over a month later, the pattern repeated further south.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.700
On March 12, nineteen-year-old Donna Gail Manson disappeared in Olympia.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.800
She was a student at The Evergreen State College who vanished while going to attend a campus jazz concert.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.000
Building a timeline for her last known whereabouts was incredibly difficult for law enforcement.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
She was not reported missing for six days.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.300
Her peers knew she frequently hitchhiked to nearby towns, so they initially assumed she had just traveled without sharing her plans.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:06.000
Detectives also knew she was depressed, meaning they could not completely rule out the slim possibility that she had left to end her own life.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.400
The disappearances kept happening.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.300
On April 17, eighteen-year-old Susan Elaine Rancourt vanished in Ellensburg.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.800
She disappeared off the Central Washington State College campus right after leaving a dorm advisors meeting.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.600
With no bodies found and very few clues to go on, law enforcement officials were baffled.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.900
Given the limits of forensic technology in the 1970s, these cases were incredibly complex to piece together.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.000
But detectives began to notice undeniable connections between the missing women.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.500
The victims shared very specific physical characteristics.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.700
They were all young, slender Caucasian women who parted their long hair in the middle.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:45.900
Healy had waist-length chestnut hair, and Manson had long dark brown hair, though Rancourt stood out slightly with blonde hair.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.500
They all came from stable backgrounds and were considered gifted or of above-average intelligence.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.300
During the times they vanished, all of them were reportedly wearing slacks.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:00.400
They were taken in the hours of darkness, always within a week of a midterm or final exam at a local college.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:07.300
Strangely enough, investigators also noted that construction work was being done on each girl's respective campus when she disappeared.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.700
The radius of the disappearances was about to expand.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.300
On May 6, twenty-year-old Oregon State University student Roberta Kathleen Parks vanished in Corvallis.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.200
She left her dormitory that evening to meet friends at a local coffee shop and never arrived.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.700
Captain Herb Swindler was convinced her disappearance was linked to the others.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.300
She had hip-length hair parted in the middle, though hers was ash blonde.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:36.100
But other law enforcement officials doubted the connection, feeling Corvallis was simply too far away from the Washington colleges.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.200
Police also knew Parks had a history of mood swings and was feeling homesick.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.300
She had recently broken up with her boyfriend.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:49.200
Just two days before she went missing, she got into a verbal altercation with her father, who suffered a near-fatal heart attack that exact same day.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.000
Because of her mental state, investigators couldn't rule out the possibility that she had taken her own life.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.600
They briefly considered the nearby Willamette River as a place of interest.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.600
The river was dragged, but searchers found absolutely nothing.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.700
Less than a month later, the timeline shifts back up to Washington state.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.300
On June 1, twenty-two-year-old Brenda Carol Ball disappeared in Burien.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.100
She was last seen leaving a place called the Flame Tavern.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.800
Like some of the earlier cases, Ball wasn't reported missing right away.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.700
She was known to have an adventurous spirit.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:22.300
People in her life thought she might have just taken off on a whim, delaying the start of any official search.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.400
Ten days later, the pattern struck the University of Washington again.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.400
Eighteen-year-old Georgann Hawkins vanished in Seattle on the night of June 11.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.300
She disappeared from an alley right behind her Kappa Alpha Theta sorority house.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:40.300
Hawkins was a former Daffodil Princess and a straight-A student who dreamed of becoming a broadcast journalist.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.000
She was petite, standing just five feet two inches tall, with long brown hair.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.700
She had a steady boyfriend at a nearby fraternity and rarely walked around the campus alone at night.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:53.200
The case file shows she had clear, concrete plans for her immediate future.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.600
She had just secured a summer job back in her hometown of Tacoma to help pay for her second year of studies.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:03.700
She intended to head home just two days after she disappeared, and her job was set to start on June 17.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.900
The missing persons reports were piling up, and the summer was just beginning.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:15.700
The missing persons reports were piling up, but the Washington disappearances ultimately culminated on a single Sunday.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.200
The date was July 14, 1974.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.800
The location was Lake Sammamish State Park.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.600
Janice Ann Ott was a twenty-three-year-old probation case worker.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.500
She was married, and she was spending the day lying on the beach.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.700
Witnesses noticed a handsome man approach her.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.900
He was wearing tennis shorts, a white shirt, and tennis shoes, and he had his arm in a sling.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:34.800
The man asked Janice for help.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.500
He told her he needed to load his sailboat onto his car, which was parked at his parents' house in Issaquah.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:45.500
Janice hesitated because she had her bicycle with her and didn't want it stolen, but the man assured her the bike would fit in his trunk.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.000
People nearby overheard the exchange.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.300
Janice agreed to help and walked away with him.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.700
She was wearing cutoff jeans and a white shirt tied over a black bikini.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:58.300
As they left, one witness heard them exchange names, with the man introducing himself as Ted.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.000
Four hours later, the exact same park became a crime scene again.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.900
Denise Marie Naslund was a nineteen-year-old computer programming student.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:10.300
She was at Lake Sammamish having a picnic with her boyfriend, another couple, and her dog.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.500
Around 4:30 that afternoon, Denise left her group to use the restroom.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:15.800
She took her dog with her.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:20.500
She was wearing cutoff shorts and a blue halter top, leaving her sandals behind on the beach.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.800
Sometime later, her dog found its way back to her friends alone.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.400
Nobody actually saw Denise leave the park with a man in a sling.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:32.000
But people who knew her told investigators she had a tender-hearted, friendly personality.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.200
They said she would have gladly helped an injured stranger without a second thought.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.100
That generated an absolute media frenzy.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:45.300
Suddenly, several other women between the ages of fifteen and twenty-six came forward with their own stories.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.600
They told police they had been approached at the park by a man named Ted who spoke with a slight British accent.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.000
He asked for help with a sailboat.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.500
One witness actually walked with him to the parking lot.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.400
She told investigators he led her to a metallic brown Volkswagen Bug.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:03.600
When she pointed out there was no sailboat on the car, he claimed it was just up the hill at his folks' house.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.900
That witness backed out, telling him she had to wait for her husband.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.000
The aftermath of Lake Sammamish changed the entire investigation.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.400
That massive media frenzy finally gave detectives a concrete profile to work with.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.500
They were no longer looking for a ghost.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:22.500
They were actively hunting this 'Ted', who used an arm sling to lure women with a fake sailboat.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.000
The case file shows just how brazen this campaign was.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:32.000
A friend of earlier victim Georgann Hawkins, a woman named Phyllis Armstrong, later shared a terrifying detail.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:38.000
She said the man had actually approached her, asking for help with his car, just a few days before Hawkins vanished.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.200
He was testing his methods in plain sight.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:48.400
But just as the public in Washington went on high alert for a man named Ted driving a brown Volkswagen, the geography of the disappearances shifted.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.900
In the summer of 1974, he moved to Salt Lake City.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.800
He enrolled as a law student at the University of Utah.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.000
The change in scenery did not slow down the violence.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.400
It actually accelerated it.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.200
Over a span of just five weeks, four more young women were murdered in Utah.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:05.000
Sixteen-year-old Nancy Wilcox vanished in Holladay.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.100
Seventeen-year-old Melissa Smith disappeared in Midvale.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.700
Laura Ann Aime, also seventeen, was taken in Lehi.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.000
And seventeen-year-old Debra Jean Kent vanished in Bountiful.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.000
What the evidence actually shows is that the tactics were evolving.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.000
He was not just relying on the injured arm routine anymore.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:26.200
On November 8, 1974, an eighteen-year-old named Carol DaRonch was at a shopping mall in Murray, Utah.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.700
A man approached her, but this time, he was posing as a police officer.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.200
He told DaRonch someone had attempted to break into her vehicle.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:38.000
He insisted she needed to come with him to the Murray Police Department to sign an official complaint.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.300
She trusted his authority, and they drove off in his Volkswagen Beetle.

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:43.700
They never made it to a police station.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.300
He eventually pulled the car over and tried to force handcuffs onto her wrists.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.100
He managed to get one cuff secured and threatened her with a gun.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.100
DaRonch fought for her life.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:55.300
She jumped out of the Volkswagen and flagged down a passing vehicle.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.800
An elderly couple picked her up and drove her straight to the authorities.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:04.300
For the first time, a victim had escaped, and she was able to give police a direct, detailed description of her attacker.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.300
Carol DaRonch escaping was a massive break in the investigation.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:12.700
She gave police a direct, detailed description of the man and his Volkswagen Beetle.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:15.800
What makes that timeline so chilling is what happened next.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.900
The record shows that was just hours after DaRonch got away.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:24.100
We already know about the string of murders that hit Utah in the late summer and fall of 1974.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:29.800
We know he was behind the disappearances of Nancy Wilcox, Melissa Smith, Laura Ann Aime, and Debra Jean Kent.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:34.300
He was balancing his legal studies with a relentless, five-week campaign of violence.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.600
By 1975, that campaign crossed state lines again.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.300
The geography expanded into Colorado and Idaho.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:47.500
On January 12, a twenty-three-year-old nurse named Caryn Campbell was vacationing with her fiancé in Snowmass Village, Colorado.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:51.900
She took the elevator up to their room while her family waited in the hotel lobby.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.300
She never came back down.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:57.500
Two months later, twenty-six-year-old Julie Cunningham was abducted in Vail.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:01.200
Then twenty-four-year-old Denise Oliverson was taken from Grand Junction.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.500
The violence was accelerating, and the victims were getting younger.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.000
In May of 1975, twelve-year-old Lynette Dawn Culver vanished in Pocatello, Idaho.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:14.500
The following month, fifteen-year-old Susan Curtis disappeared while walking home from a banquet in Provo, Utah.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:20.400
Law enforcement agencies across multiple states were dealing with missing women, but they didn't have a name to connect them.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.000
That finally changed in the early hours of August 16, 1975.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:28.000
Utah Highway Patrol Sergeant Bob Hayward was finishing his shift in Granger.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.600
He spotted a Volkswagen Beetle parked in front of a house where he knew two young women lived.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:34.500
He flipped on his bright headlights.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:36.200
The driver immediately took off.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:39.500
Sergeant Hayward chased the vehicle down and pulled it over.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:41.100
He started questioning the driver.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:45.100
That's when he noticed the passenger seat of the Volkswagen had been completely removed.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.700
He also searched a gym bag inside the car.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.800
The contents looked like a burglary kit.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.500
Hayward found rope, a ski mask, gloves, and a crowbar.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:57.300
He found an ice pick, a pair of handcuffs, and pantyhose with holes cut out for the eyes and nose.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.800
The driver was arrested for evading an officer.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.800
The law student was finally in custody.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.300
We already know about the eighteen-year-old who fought for her life in a Volkswagen Beetle.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:10.900
Carol DaRonch escaping that fake police officer in Murray was the thread that finally unraveled this case.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:16.400
Remember, she flagged down that elderly couple just hours before Debra Jean Kent disappeared.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:20.000
She gave investigators a clear description of the driver and his car.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.800
Now, in August of 1975, Utah Highway Patrol had that exact car impounded.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.200
The driver was sitting in a jail cell.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:29.800
Detectives organized a police lineup and brought DaRonch in to take a look.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:35.000
The case file notes that he actually made attempts to alter his appearance before standing in that lineup.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:35.700
It did not work.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:39.900
DaRonch looked at the men and identified him as the person who attacked her.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:44.800
On October 2, he was officially charged with aggravated kidnapping and attempted criminal assault.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:49.400
The man who had been hunting young women across multiple states was finally facing a bench trial.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.700
That trial took place in 1976.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:53.300
The evidence held up in court.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:14:00.000
He was found guilty of aggravated kidnapping and sentenced to serve between one and fifteen years in a Utah state prison.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:04.800
While he was sitting in that cell, the broader investigation was gaining momentum.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:10.200
Sheriffs from neighboring counties, including up in Washington, were looking closely at the Utah inmate.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:16.200
They strongly suspected he was the same man responsible for the string of unsolved murders in the Pacific Northwest.

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:19.600
But it was Colorado that made the first definitive move.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:25.000
Investigators executed a search of his impounded Volkswagen Beetle and found hairs inside the vehicle.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.600
The record shows that one of those hairs was a match to Caryn Campbell.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:31.100
She was found murdered near Snowmass in February.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:36.800
Based on that physical evidence, he was officially charged with first-degree murder in Campbell's death.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.600
Authorities transferred him from the Utah state prison to a jail in Aspen, Colorado, to stand trial.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.200
He was granted permission to assist in his own defense.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:47.300
That decision came with unprecedented privileges.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:52.700
The judge actually decreed that the defendant did not need to wear leg shackles or handcuffs in the courthouse.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:57.000
He was allowed to walk freely into the courtroom and up to the second-floor law library.

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:00.500
What the case file adds are the details of that first escape.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:03.400
It took roughly ten minutes for anyone to realize he was gone.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:10.200
Police threw up roadblocks at both ends of Aspen and searched every single vehicle leaving town, but he had already fled into the mountains.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.400
He broke into a cabin and hid there for several days.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:19.400
Eventually, he walked right back into Aspen and stole a car that had been left unlocked with the keys in the ignition.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:23.200
A deputy spotted the stolen car weaving along the road and pulled it over.

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:26.300
Six days after jumping out the window, he was back in custody.

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:29.200
He immediately started planning his next move.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.900
The record shows he dropped twenty to twenty-five pounds.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:36.400
His cell had an unsecured grate in the ceiling and a light fixture that had not been welded shut.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.900
He used that oversight to his advantage.

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:42.200
He carved the opening in the ceiling wider so he could fit through.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:47.600
On the night of December 30, he arranged law books and pillows under his blankets to look like a sleeping body.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:54.600
Then he crawled up into the ductwork, dropped down into an empty jailer's apartment, put on civilian clothes, and walked out into the night.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:59.300
What the case file adds is that Caryn Campbell was a nurse visiting from Dearborn, Michigan.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:02.400
She was staying at the Wildwood Inn with her fiancé and two children.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.900
She stepped into a hotel elevator and was simply gone.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:09.000
The timeline shows the violence in Colorado moving incredibly fast.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.000
You know Julie was abducted on March 15.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.900
The record points to April 6 for the next disappearance.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:15.400
That is when Denise was taken.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:19.700
Investigators later learned Oliverson was killed in a secluded location.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:23.700
Three women were taken from three different Colorado towns in under three months.

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:29.400
The geography kept shifting, making it nearly impossible for local authorities to see the bigger picture at the time.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.800
The violence eventually pushed back into Idaho and Utah.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.100
We know Lynette vanished.

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:39.500
What the notes add is that he eventually confessed to taking the girl to his motel room and killing her there.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:41.100
We also know Susan disappeared.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.300
Her body was never found.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:45.600
He waited until the eve of his execution to confess to her murder.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:50.300
After he escaped Colorado and made it to Tallahassee, the violence exploded.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:56.700
On the night of January 15, 1978, he attacked four women at the Chi Omega sorority house at Florida State University.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:17:00.500
He assaulted another student just blocks away that exact same night.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:07.600
He was eventually charged with three counts of attempted murder for attacking Karen Chandler, Cheryl Thomas, and Kathy Kleiner.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.000
The trials were a media spectacle.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:12.900
The first murder trial opened in Miami on June 11, 1979.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:18.300
It was moved from Tallahassee because pretrial publicity had completely saturated the local jury pool.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:22.700
The Florida Supreme Court had recently authorized cameras in courtrooms statewide.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:25.200
The judge denied a request to block the coverage.

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:29.500
It became one of the first nationally televised murder trials in the United States.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:33.000
He actually represented himself for portions of that trial.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.400
He cross-examined witnesses.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:36.900
The strategy did little to undercut the prosecution.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.100
The most gripping piece of evidence was a bite mark left on Lisa Levy.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:43.300
Investigators had photographed the wound to scale at the crime scene.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:46.900
Three months later, they got a search warrant to compel dental impressions.

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:52.500
A forensic odontologist named Dr. Richard Souviron made wax and stone molds of his mouth.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:58.900
The molds showed a set of lower teeth that were twisted, turned, and jammed together in a very distinctive pattern.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:04.000
At trial, prosecutors put enlarged photographs of the impressions right next to the wound.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:05.400
The pattern was a match.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:11.100
On July 24, 1979, the jury convicted him of two counts of first-degree murder and two counts of burglary.

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.000
He received two death sentences.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.300
The second trial began in Orlando in January 1980.

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.600
This was for the murder of Kimberly Leach.

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.700
He did not represent himself this time.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.100
The jury deliberated for less than ten hours.

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:26.300
They convicted him of kidnapping and first-degree murder.

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:29.400
He received his third death sentence on February 10, 1980.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.700
In his final days, he confessed to approximately thirty murders across seven states.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:38.600
He gave the state-by-state details to FBI Special Agent Bill Hagmaier.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:43.800
On January 24, 1989, he was strapped into the electric chair at Florida State Prison in Starke.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:46.300
He was pronounced dead at 7:16 in the morning.

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:49.800
We know it reached those states by the late spring of 1975.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.200
The timeline in the case file shows exactly how fast this happened.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:56.100
Between January and April, the geography shifted again.

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:58.800
On May 6, the pattern hit Pocatello, Idaho.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.600
That is the exact date she vanished.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:05.800
As I mentioned earlier, he later confessed to abducting her and ending her life inside his motel room.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.000
Less than two months later, the hunt moved back to Utah.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:11.500
On the night of June 27, she was attending a banquet.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:14.000
She disappeared while walking home from that event.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:19.800
What the evidence actually shows is a man targeting increasingly younger victims just weeks before his first arrest.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:25.700
That June disappearance was the final one before Sergeant Bob Hayward pulled over that Volkswagen Beetle in August.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:30.400
The timeline was accelerating right up until the moment he was caught with the ski mask and handcuffs.

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:34.700
We already know how the traffic stop in Granger played out on August 16, 1975.

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:39.200
You know Hayward pulled over that fleeing Volkswagen and found the passenger seat missing.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:44.100
We talked about the gym bag inside holding the ice pick, the handcuffs, and the altered pantyhose.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:46.800
We also know the direct result of that arrest.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:50.300
The charges came after Carol DaRonch picked him out of a police lineup.

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:55.400
That identification gave them the thread they needed to start tying him to the unsolved 1974 murders.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:00.200
But the case file reveals something incredible about the timing of that Granger arrest.

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:02.700
Detectives in Seattle were drowning in paperwork.

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:08.500
The sheer volume of the Washington investigation had completely outgrown what human investigators could sort by hand.

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:13.100
Law enforcement was looking at 41,000 registered owners of Volkswagens.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:20.200
They had to cross-reference those vehicles against a list of roughly 3,500 possible suspects in the Washington disappearances.

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:26.000
They decided to use a computer to sort through more than 300,000 names to produce a ranked list of suspects.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:28.700
That computer program actually worked.

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:31.400
It generated a shortlist of the most likely suspects.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:35.000
The law student sitting in a Utah jail cell came up seventh on that list.

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:38.900
A correct answer sitting at number seven is still a correct answer.

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:43.800
The technology had successfully narrowed down hundreds of thousands of files to a handful of men.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:49.100
But before Seattle detectives could act on that ranking, a routine traffic stop in Granger beat them to it.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:55.000
The gap between identifying a suspect on paper and physically tracking him down is where the danger lives.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:01.200
If Sergeant Hayward had not noticed that parked car, or if the driver had not sped off, the timeline might look very different.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:06.100
The computer had the name, but a patrolman on a late shift is what actually took him off the streets.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.800
The fallout from that traffic stop moved quickly.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:14.900
Because of the massive media presence surrounding the case, the defendant actually requested a bench trial for the kidnapping charge.

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:17.600
That meant a judge would decide the verdict instead of a jury.

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:20.300
The trial began on February 23, 1976.

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:23.400
The prosecution built their case around two key people.

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:31.200
They relied on Carol DaRonch detailing her escape, and they relied on Sergeant Hayward explaining exactly what he found in that gym bag.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:32.900
The strategy worked.

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:35.700
A week later, on March 1, the judge found him guilty.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:40.800
That conviction is what finally put him in a state prison with a sentence of one to fifteen years.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:46.100
We already know he was transferred to Colorado to stand trial for the murder of Caryn Campbell.

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:49.400
What the case file adds is the timeline of those legal proceedings.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:53.000
In late January 1977, he was officially extradited to face the charges.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:55.600
He pleaded not guilty and acted as his own attorney.

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:59.000
The preliminary hearing started in early April of that year.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.600
It dragged on for several weeks.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:06.100
We know he used that time to access the courthouse law library, leading up to that June 7 escape during a court recess.

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:10.500
We talked about how he hid in a cabin before getting caught in a stolen car.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:13.400
What the notes add is his own account of that first escape.

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:17.900
He later told a man named Carlisle that he had no money, no help, and absolutely no plan.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:24.300
He spent those six days wandering aimlessly in the wilderness, exhausted and hungry, before returning to Aspen on June 13.

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:27.300
He dropped the weight to fit through a ceiling grate.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:32.500
His former defense attorney, John Henry Browne, actually visited him there and noticed the drastic weight loss.

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:35.100
Browne later said he wondered why his client was doing it.

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:37.700
The answer came on the night of December 30.

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:43.100
Browne later compared the breakout to a movie, noting how his former client crawled right through the ducting.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:46.000
We already discussed the cross-country trip that followed.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.700
But the record shows exactly how he got out of Colorado.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:54.100
After breaking into the jailer's closet and stealing civilian clothes, he walked out the front gate.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:57.400
He found another vehicle to steal and drove it all the way to Denver.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:22:59.600
That is where he boarded the flight to Chicago.

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:02.300
He was a fugitive with a massive head start.

00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:08.000
He managed to successfully flee across the country, landing in Florida weeks before the murder of Kimberly Leach.

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:13.500
The case file shows how investigators built the timeline leading up to that final abduction.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:18.100
Columbia County prosecutors, led by George R. Dekle, Sr., worked to trace his movements.

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:20.500
They taped a statement from a man named Farhat.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.900
He owned a Jacksonville sporting goods store.

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:29.200
He claimed he sold the fugitive a Buck knife for twenty-six dollars on February 8, just one day before Kimberly vanished.

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:33.400
What the record adds is the sheer volume of that physical evidence.

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:37.500
Those fibers were all over Kimberly's socks, her jeans, her bra, and her purse.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.300
They also matched her blood to a bloodstain discovered inside the van.

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:44.700
That evidence was presented in Orlando in January 1980.

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:48.600
The trial was originally scheduled to take place in Live Oak but was moved.

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:53.600
His defense lawyer, Julius Africano, tried to dismiss the evidence in his opening statement.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:56.700
He called the prosecution's case a chain of circumstances.

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:03.700
The prosecutors answered that by putting Kimberly's mother, Freda Leach, on the stand to testify about the day her daughter disappeared.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:05.300
Then they brought in Jacques Moore.

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:08.700
He was one of three eyewitnesses who placed the defendant near the school.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:12.400
The chain of circumstances was actually a web of physical and eyewitness proof.

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:17.000
We know a twelve-year-old girl was taken from her junior high school on February 9, 1978.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:22.000
But when you look at how the public actually reacted that morning, the record gets incredibly frustrating.

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:27.800
One witness was a city firefighter who saw that white van blocking traffic around 8:30 in the morning.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:31.500
He watched a man lead a distraught young girl toward the passenger side.

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:34.900
The girl was wearing a football jersey, and she was clearly upset.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:38.200
That witness was an emergency medical technician.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:40.800
He was literally trained to help people in crisis.

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:44.500
But the case file shows he decided he simply did not want to get involved.

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:48.800
He waited a full six months before finally reporting what he saw to the authorities.

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:51.900
That delay was incredibly unhelpful to a desperate search.

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:56.300
Other people on the street that morning also saw the van circling the school.

00:24:56.300 --> 00:25:01.600
A crossing guard noticed the driver staring hard at the building multiple times over the course of an hour.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:08.000
Another driver was nearly run off the highway by a white van, noting the man behind the wheel was bobbing his head with a slack jaw.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:13.100
By the time the suspect was arrested in Pensacola, the local press was swarming the story.

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:17.000
The coverage completely saturated Lake City and the surrounding counties.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:25.200
Public interest exploded when authorities held a press conference to announce the fugitive had checked out of a local motel on the exact morning of the abduction.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:29.900
Law enforcement continued to feed that media frenzy instead of keeping the details quiet.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:34.400
A local sheriff went on the record to tell reporters exactly what the state crime lab was testing.

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:38.100
He publicly discussed the large bloodstain found on the carpet of the stolen van.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:43.700
The news coverage even detailed how sand had been tossed over that bloodstain to try and conceal it.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:48.100
Reporters broadcasted the theory that the soil came from a nearby river flood plain.

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:53.500
The public was getting a real-time, unfiltered look at a horrific crime scene before a trial even started.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:57.100
It was a circus, and it complicated an already fragile investigation.

00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:01.000
We already know a Pensacola officer spotted that stolen Volkswagen.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.400
What the case file adds is how violent that traffic stop actually got.

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:09.000
Patrolman David Lee originally pulled the car over because the driver had his headlights off.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:15.800
When Lee realized the vehicle was stolen, he ordered the man out, but the suspect punched the officer right in the face and took off running.

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:19.700
Lee chased him down, aiming his revolver and shouting for him to stop.

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:22.700
The officer fired a single shot, and the fugitive collapsed.

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:24.700
Lee thought he had incapacitated the man.

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:27.800
But as he approached, the suspect lunged for the officer's gun.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.100
They fought for control of the weapon.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:33.300
Lee had to pistol-whip the man three times just to get him in restraints.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.300
The fight was completely drained out of him.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:40.400
During the drive to the police station, the suspect looked at the officer and said he wished Lee had just killed him.

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:43.500
We talked about how he eventually dropped his fake ID.

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:48.800
The record shows he only revealed his true identity in exchange for a phone call to his girlfriend in Seattle.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:52.900
Detective Norman Chapman practically begged him to help them find Kimberly's body.

00:26:52.900 --> 00:26:56.600
The man refused, telling Chapman the site was simply too horrible to look at.

00:26:57.100 --> 00:26:59.300
The search dragged on for nearly two months.

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:00.700
It finally ended on April 7.

00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:06.900
That is when Kimberly's remains were discovered in that abandoned hog shed, located roughly thirty miles west of Lake City.

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:09.900
We know the Orlando jury convicted him in 1980.

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:14.900
He spent the rest of the decade filing multiple unsuccessful appeals from Florida State Prison.

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:17.100
He tried to use a bones-for-time strategy.

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:22.500
He purposely withheld the locations of victims, hoping to trade those secrets to delay his execution.

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:27.100
Governor Rob Martinez signed the final death warrant on January 17, 1989.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:33.400
The inmate spent his final days talking to people like detective Robert Keppel and FBI profiler William Hagmaier.

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:39.100
He did a taped interview with Reverend James Dobson, blaming pornography for shaping his violent urges.

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:42.300
He even confessed to necrophilia for the very first time.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:45.800
But he completely refused to talk about Kimberly Leach.

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:50.500
He held onto that secret until his last-minute clemency request was finally rejected.

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:55.200
After years of absolute denial, he confessed to her murder on the eve of his execution.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:56.400
Read the file.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:57.600
Form your own conclusion.

00:27:57.600 --> 00:27:58.600
That's it for this one.

